[Music]  so  [Music]  [Applause]  [Music]  hello my name is cecil slipovic and i'm  musician in residence at yale  university's  fortune of video archive for holocaust  testimonies  this video series is designed as a  companion to our album  where is our homeland which is the first  volume in the archives ongoing effort  to present songs and poems performed by  survivors in testimonies given to the  fortune of archive  over the last 40 years you can learn  more about the project on the archives  website  songs from testimonies.org  where is our homeland songs from  testimonies volume 1  was recorded in 2019 by the cecil  slupovich ensemble  featuring renowned singer sasha luria  craig udelman on the violin joshua camp  on the accordion  keyboards and guitar dmitry ischenko on  the base  and myself on woodwinds the songs in  this collection differ vastly in terms  of style  social and historical background  language and many other aspects  but collectively they create a  multi-dimensional picture  of jewish life before and during world  war  ii today we'll talk about  the song that was written in german  although the name is in hebrew kadima  forward thorvalds in german and  it was sung by itzhak as  itzhak was born in 1915 in berlin  germany  he actively participated in jewish  zionist youth groups  and according to his testimony  established  one himself in the interview  given in hebrew itzhak recalls the song  allegedly composed by akiva lewinsky  born in 1918 and died in 2000  a prominent zionist leader here's a  short clip from his testimony  [Music]  is  [Music]  forward is written in the genre of a  solemn probably military march typical  for the anthems  and are the officially sponsored songs  in europe and the soviet union  in the 30s very few of the ideologically  charged songs be the zionists buddhist  or communist  were musically dramatically jewish the  composers  most of them are known to us and the  survivors themselves were part of the  multi-ethnic  multilingual and ultimately cosmopolitan  milieu  in which they grew up  [Applause]  [Music]  yes foreign  [Music]  [Applause]  [Music]  machine  [Music]  is  [Music]  [Applause]  this  [Music]  [Applause]  [Music]  when yitzhak refers to this song he  describes it as a  hymn or a hymnan in hebrew that was sung  by members of his german jewish youth  group when they were in  hiding or when they were living as an  underground during the second world war  but he does acknowledge that it was  written earlier during the weimar period  so let's talk a little bit about this  song and what it tells us about german  zionism  during the 1920s and the early 30s well  first of all who wrote it  itzhak and his testimony attributes it  to  akiva levinsky who was an important  figure in the state of israel  during its first four decades he was a  functionary in the jewish agency  he was the director of bankapolium and  he did a lot of other important things  so was he the guy well we do know  that according to his biography he  joined a german jewish youth group  when he was a child when he was nine  evidently  and that it was called kadima well  kadeem is the name of the song  we also know that he wrote music and  poetry as a youth  evidently he wrote poems from early  childhood  he was also a great aficionado of hebrew  from an early age and was committed to  moving to the land of israel  uh but there we have a little bit of a  problem in that he moved  to the land of israel in 1934 when he  was 14 years old  so if he wrote this song and he might  very well have he did it when he was  10 11 12 13 years old now it is a simple  song  so it is possible that he wrote it then  and in the testimony his haqq sings it  in both hebrew and the german and i  wonder if it was written in both  languages  so we can't be 100 sure that lewinsky  wrote it  but i think there's a good chance that  he did it was just a very gifted child  let me say a few words about the song  itself in its broader context it's it's  a very german song  in its cadence and it's music and in its  language  some of it's a bit disturbing like the  reference to ainoya stakes  you know a new and strong breed of  humanity but to be fair  that kind of language was used not only  in the german  world in the 1920s but what's  interesting also is the song doesn't  really say anything specifically about  jews  uh or about judaism or about aliyah  or about arabs israel but there is  something it refers to twice it refers  to honor or era the yudus  or the era of israel that is the jewish  people  and that's not a coincidence the zionist  movement from the very beginning was  very concerned with  with honor and one doesn't have to look  only a german zionism for this  lev pinsker who was from odessa  published a famous pamphlet in 1882  called self-emancipation and it was very  much about honor  but how the jews need to honor  themselves so they can be honored by  the rest of the world honor and respect  were central  in the zionist writings of taylor herzl  so  this is a theme that goes back you know  long before this song  was written and indeed there were german  zionist  hymns of the 19  teens the turn of the century and even  earlier  tater herzl's favorite zionist hymn had  been written in 1885 but it was quite  popular in his own time  and it has language that in some ways is  quite different from the song we're  looking at  but there's a certain commonality i want  to read just a few lines from this poem  it was called  there where the cedar and it begins like  this  there where the slender cedar kisses the  clouds and where the jordan's rapids  flow  there where my father's ashes rest the  earth  has drunk the blood of maccabees  now the reference to maccabees is  important here akiva levinsky  we know that he belonged to a youth  group called kadima which is the name of  this song  well that youth group merged with  another group called sophie  or scouts and it formed a new group  called  young maccabees comparing jews to  maccabees to ancient warriors was found  throughout modern jewish history  but it's particularly powerful in uh  german zionism  and it goes back to 1914 when the  economist and sociologist  franz oppenheimer wrote an essay called  ito annoya  macabe old and new maccabees  now let me now uh talk a little bit  about the background of german zionism  of the 1920s uh on the first tape  of the testimony there's five different  tapes but on the first one itzhak refers  to himself  joining kodima uh when he was a child  now this was an organization that grew  out of a more famous  german jewish youth group called lavis  or blue white that had been founded in  1912.  and it collapsed in 1926 and internal  politics and  youth groups and often sort of came and  went in weimar germany  but what happened over time first in  blauvis and then kodima  is that the movement's focus changed  like the german youth movement as a  whole in the earlier 20th century the  emphasis was on individual cultivation  personal experience with nature long  rambles through the forests  music and so forth in germany and among  german jewish youth as well  as we get further into the 1920s and the  early 30s  the youth movements become more  nationalistic and for the jewish youth  movements a greater emphasis  not just on zionism but on aliyah on  immigration to eretz israel  and preparation for life in  the land of israel as a laborer  particularly on ikebutz  kadima was part of a whole umbrella  group of  german jewish youth groups that had  about 2 000 young people in all  and i just want to read you a sentence  from the founding statement of this  umbrella organization  because it really gives you a sense of  what german youth groups  german jewish youth groups were all  about when you get into the later  twenties  we firmly believe that in palestine the  cornerstone for a new society is being  laid  we cannot do a thing here except to  prepare ourselves  to become worthy helpers of the  palestinian workers  and to teach the german jewish youth the  ideal of a new and dignified life in  eretz israel  note the emphasis not only on labor but  also on dignity which gets us back to  honor that i talked about earlier now  how we judge the seriousness of these  statements really depends on  perspective there were um  about 2 000 german jewish youth in these  organizations  uh hundreds of them did wind up moving  to eretz israel in the 1920s  about 2 000 german jews and all moved  to eretz israel that might not sound  like many  but there were 20 000 german jews uh in  the syrian easter shift  line the the german zionist organization  and that's actually quite a lot  when we think about the jewish  population of germany being about half a  million  in the united states which had eight  times as many jews more than four  million jews  there were about 60 000 jews total  in the american zionist organizations so  you know three times as many as in  germany but the community was eight  times larger  and also the american community was  about  13 000 members of the american zionist  organization and the rest were in  hadassah the women's zionist  organizations it was split  immigration to the land of israel was  low so actually german zionism was much  more committed to errors israel  than um american zionism and german  zionism was more  popular in germany than american zionism  was in the united states  and that might be counterintuitive but i  think it's important to keep that in  mind  the german jewish world that gave birth  to this song then was one where zionism  was pretty prevalent  uh not dominant prevalent and within  zionism there was a serious commitment  to immigration even if not  um all the time realized in principle  the fact that the song itself doesn't  refer to the land of israel is a bit of  a red herring  it may have been written by again a very  young man still a boy  whose own thinking was in the process of  formation  or perhaps if it was written by someone  else it was written at a time when the  youth movement was undergoing  transformations and debates about how  important erotism would be to it  all this to say that we can learn an  awful lot from a little song  [Music]  bye

